      "problem": "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)",
